Notes

- The computation factors over primes p. For primes not dividing any Bi, F_p = 1 and can be ignored.
- For each prime p, bi = v_p(Bi) defines the per-prime transitions.
- The DP keeps track of whether a zero has appeared (to enforce gcd = 1) and accumulates the weight p^{sum a_i} along the path.
- Overall runtime is about O(M · sum_i Ω(Bi)), which for the given constraints stays well within limits.

Idea:
- The supervision graph is a functional graph (each node points to exactly one supervisor). Each connected component consists of one cycle with trees feeding into cycle nodes.
- On a cycle, all cycle nodes must have the same grade (g_i ≤ g_{S_i} around the cycle implies equality around the cycle).
- For each non-cycle node, consider its subtree consisting of all non-cycle nodes that eventually reach that node. If a node v has children c1..cm (non-cycle nodes), then for a given parent cap t, the number of ways for v’s subtree is:
  F_v(t) = sum_{g=1..t} prod_{children u of v} F_u(g)
  The value for a leaf is F_leaf(t) = t.
- We can compute F_v for all non-cycle nodes bottom-up by removing leaves (a Kahn-like process). While removing a non-cycle node v, we already know F_v, so we update its parent p by multiplying into P[p][g] (the product of F_child(g) over processed children). When all children of p are processed, p itself becomes eligible to be removed.
- After removing all non-cycle nodes, for every cycle node v, P[v][x] equals the number of ways its attached non-cycle subtree contributes given v has grade x. For a cycle with nodes V, the number of valid assignments is:
  sum_{x=1..T} prod_{v in V} P[v][x]
- The total answer is the product of these cycle contributions over all cycles.

Complexities: O((K + number_of_cycles) * T) time and O(K * T) memory, which fits K,T ≤ 2025.

C++17 implementation:

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

static const long long MOD = 998244353LL;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int K, T;
    if (!(cin >> K >> T)) return 0;
    vector<int> S(K);
    for (int i = 0; i < K; ++i) {
        int x; cin >> x;
        S[i] = x - 1; // 0-based
    }

    // indegree is number of children of a node (including self-edge)
    vector<int> indeg(K, 0);
    for (int i = 0; i < K; ++i) indeg[S[i]]++;

    // P[v][g] = product of F_child(g) over processed non-cycle children of v
    vector<vector<long long>> P(K, vector<long long>(T + 1, 1)); // index 1..T

    queue<int> q;
    for (int i = 0; i < K; ++i) if (indeg[i] == 0) q.push(i);

    vector<char> removed(K, false); // non-cycle processed
    while (!q.empty()) {
        int v = q.front(); q.pop();
        removed[v] = true;

        // Compute F_v(t) = sum_{g=1}^t P[v][g]
        vector<long long> Fv(T + 1, 0);
        long long acc = 0;
        for (int t = 1; t <= T; ++t) {
            acc += P[v][t];
            if (acc >= MOD) acc -= MOD;
            Fv[t] = acc;
        }

        int p = S[v];
        // Propagate to parent p
        for (int g = 1; g <= T; ++g) {
            P[p][g] = (P[p][g] * Fv[g]) % MOD;
        }

        indeg[p]--;
        if (indeg[p] == 0) q.push(p);
    }

    // Process cycles
    vector<char> vis(K, false);
    long long ans = 1;

    for (int i = 0; i < K; ++i) {
        if (indeg[i] > 0 && !vis[i]) {
            // collect cycle
            vector<int> cyc;
            int u = i;
            do {
                cyc.push_back(u);
                vis[u] = true;
                u = S[u];
            } while (!vis[u]);

            // A_cycle = sum_{x=1..T} prod_{v in cyc} P[v][x]
            long long A = 0;
            for (int x = 1; x <= T; ++x) {
                long long prod = 1;
                for (int vtx : cyc) {
                    prod = (prod * P[vtx][x]) % MOD;
                }
                A += prod;
                if (A >= MOD) A -= MOD;
            }
            ans = (ans * A) % MOD;
        }
    }

    cout << ans % MOD << '\n';
    return 0;
}
